  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 251 PM CDT Sat Sep 5 2026\n\n(Tuesday - Wednesday)\n\nModel guidance shifts the anomalous mid/upper back toward the mid-\nMississippi Valley for midweek. Temperatures will warm back up each \nday through Wednesday as a result, though relative humidity values \nTuesday will still be low. The spread on the timing of the midweek \ncold front has lowered, and now looks like late Wednesday night into \nearly Thursday. The hottest day will be ahead of this front, with \nhighs Wednesday back in the upper 90s to near 100 degrees, again \nthreatening daily records. Heat index values may approach 105 \ndegrees as well in some parts of the area, though the confidence in \nhow widespread these values will be is low at this time range.\n\n\n(Wednesday Night - Next Saturday)\n\nThe best chances (40-60%) of showers and thunderstorms is associated \nwith the frontal boundary, though some weak low/mid level \nfrontogenesis lags the surface front, which may prolong the threat. \nThese rain chances also are likely to come up as the differences in \nthe frontal timing decrease in the coming days. Chances for \nmeasurable rain on the LREF over a 24-hour period ending Thursday \nevening are 60-80%. There is skepticism on widespread significant \nrainfall at this time, but any rain would be a welcome sight given \nthe dryness over the past 2+ weeks. Probabilities for 0.25\"+ of rain \nare only in the 25-40% range along/north of I-70 for reference.\n\nBehind the cold front, highs in the mid to upper 80s are expected \nfor Thursday and Friday (Thursday potentially being aided by cloud \ncover and rain). While still about 5 degrees above normal, this may \nbe the first time below 90 degrees for many areas. \n\nUncertainty in the evolution of the pattern across the CONUS \nincreases heading toward next weekend. While there is still going to \nbe an omega block, where the trough-ridge-trough pattern sets up is \nthe question mark. Three of the four clusters of the 500-hPa height \npattern from the LREF have our area beneath zonal or northwest flow \naloft (i.e., the center of the ridge is to our south or southwest). \nThis in theory could yield either another cold frontal passage \nsometime or a \"ridge-runner\" shortwave and rain chances. One cluster \nhas the amplified ridge right over us, which would mean hotter and \ndry weather. This cluster though only comprises 20% of the LREF \nmembership, so this scenario is not favored at this time.",
